<header><p class="st-kicker">nomadic confederation · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Oghuz Yabgu State</h1><p class="st-meta">750 CE – 1055 CE (approx.) · capital Yangikent</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Oghuz Turkic state of the lower Syr Darya and Aral steppe, cradle of the Seljuks and of the Turkmen.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghuz-turks">Oghuz</a> tribes moved from the eastern steppe to the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aral-sea">Aral</a> region in the 8th century, after the fall of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-turkic-khaganate">Western Turkic Khaganate</a> and wars with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/karluk-yabgu">Karluks</a>. By the 9th century they formed a confederation led by a ruler with the title yabghu.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The yabghu ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangikent">Yangikent</a> near the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a>, a winter capital among nomadic pastures. The traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-fadlan">Ibn Fadlan</a>, who crossed their lands in 922, described their customs, military leaders and lack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. They traded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/samanid-empire">Samanid Empire</a>, and fought the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a>. Islam spread among them in the 10th century.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Internal splits weakened the state; the Seljuk clan broke away to the south and conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> from 1037. Around the mid-11th century the remaining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghuz-turks">Oghuz</a> were pushed west by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kipchaks">Kipchaks</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kimek-khanate">Kimek Khanate</a>, some becoming the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/torks">Torks</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russian steppe</a>, others the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/oghuz-yabgu-state.jpg" alt="Oghuz Yabgu State" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Adaykz /; derivative work by Barefact · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AD_750OguzYabgu.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>750 CE – 1055 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>nomadic confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Yangikent</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ali Khan, Shah Malik</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tengrism, later Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Oghuz Turkic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>, <a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, <a href="/countries/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Oghuz Yabghu, Oghuz state</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=902&amp;focus=oghuz-yabgu-state" aria-label="Oghuz Yabgu State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6167 619 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Oghuz Yabgu State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6487 875L6481 1127"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6481 1127h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6487 875h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6487 875h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=902&amp;focus=oghuz-yabgu-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
